    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d19">

                <pb facs="0095" n="65" xml:id="pg65"/>

                <head>19. Letter From the President’s Special Assistant for National Security
                    Affairs (Cutler) to Secretary of State Dulles<anchor n="0" xml:id="d19-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1958-04-07">April 7,
                    1958</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Foster</hi>: Following the conference in Neil’s
                    office this morning, I am sending you two papers (reflecting my own personal
                    views) germane to the subject under discussion: <list>
                        <label>(1)</label>
                        <item>Memorandum entitled “Some Elements for a Realistic National Military
                            Strategy in a Time of Maximum Tension and Distrust” (Top Secret and For
                            Your Information Only), April 7, 1958.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d19-f2"/>
                            This memorandum represents my first attempt to write down these
                            views.</item>
                        <label>(2)</label>
                        <item>Memorandum entitled “Massive Exchange of Nuclear Weapons” (Copy No. 2
                            of 7 Copies, Secret and Eyes Only), March 16, 1958.<anchor n="2"
                                xml:id="d19-f3"/> I have reviewed this memorandum with a very few
                            friends at my level or higher in Government, and with them discussed it
                            one day with the President. Subsequently, the President directed the NSC
                            Net Evaluation Subcommittee to conduct the 1958 Net Evaluation upon a
                            targeting plan directed to non-military targets with a view to
                            paralyzing the enemy nation.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>I am furnishing similar material to Secretary McElroy.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Robert Cutler</hi><anchor n="3"
                            xml:id="d19-f4"/></signed></closer>




                <xi:include href="d19-a1.xml" xpointer="d19-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d19-a2.xml" xpointer="d19-a2"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d19-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d19-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Eisenhower Library, White House Office Files, Project Clean Up, Nuclear
                    Policy. Top Secret. A copy was sent to Goodpaster. The source text is attached
                    to a brief summary, also by Cutler, of the meeting described in <ref
                        target="#d18">Document 18</ref>. See the Supplement.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d19-f2" n="1" xml:id="d19-f2-content">
                <p>Printed below as Attachment 1. Also attached was an undated “Summary of
                    Conclusions” (printed below as Attachment 2), and a draft memorandum dated April
                    7 and entitled “Some Elements of a National Military Strategy in a Time of
                    Maximum Tension, Distrust and Destructive Capability.” The draft memorandum is
                    in the Supplement.</p>
            </note>
